### What happened?

Aborting a bash command whose detached descendant keeps writing through the **inherited** `stdout`/`stderr` pipes never settles. The abort path in `createLocalShellOperations()` (`dist/core/tools/bash.js`) calls `killProcessTree(child.pid)` but does not close the local pipe ends. `waitForChildProcess()` deliberately re-arms its post-exit idle timer on every arriving chunk (the idle-drain behavior from #5303), so an escaped descendant that writes continuously keeps re-arming that timer forever: the killed command group is gone, yet the tool call stays pending indefinitely — until the descendant happens to exit on its own.

`AbortController.abort()` on the tool signal, safety-limit expiry, and pre-handoff cancellation all hit this path, so the pending tool result blocks the turn instead of rejecting with the existing `aborted` error.

### Minimal reproduction

pi 0.85.1, Linux (or macOS with any detached-writer variant):

```js
import { createLocalBashOperations } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const ops = createLocalBashOperations();
const controller = new AbortController();
const started = Date.now();

const pending = ops.exec(
  "setsid node -e 'setInterval(() => process.stdout.write(\"x\\n\"), 20)' & wait",
  process.cwd(),
  { onData: () => {}, signal: controller.signal },
);
setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), 250);

try {
  await pending;
} catch (error) {
  console.error(error.message, `${((Date.now() - started) / 1000).toFixed(1)}s after abort`);
}
```

**Expected:** the exec rejects with the existing `aborted` error promptly after the abort — bounded output cleanup after cancellation.

**Actual:** the promise stays pending indefinitely (the `setsid` writer never stops writing), so nothing is logged and the aborted tool call never settles.

The same holds for the timeout path when no explicit `timeout` is set and cleanup relies on the group kill alone — #6787 tracks the timeout side of this.

### Proposed behavior

Route abort and timeout through one shared termination helper inside `createLocalShellOperations()`:

1. Kill the original command group immediately via the existing `killProcessTree` (unchanged).
2. After a short grace period (e.g. 500 ms) destroy the local `stdout`/`stderr` pipe ends so `waitForChildProcess()` settles, releasing the executor.
3. Schedule the pipe-cleanup timer at most once when abort and timeout overlap, and clear it in the existing `finally` cleanup when the executor settles first.
4. Keep the abort/timeout error messages and their precedence, the already-aborted-signal handling, and the normal-completion idle-drain behavior from #5303 unchanged.

This bounds pipe cleanup after cancellation of the original command group; it does not attempt to terminate descendants that escape that group (they simply lose the inherited pipes).

### Plan

I intend to submit a PR with the equivalent source change and regression tests for: an escaped descendant continuously writing stdout, one writing stderr, abort/timeout overlap (single cleanup, unchanged error precedence), and normal completion preserving post-exit descendant output.

### What happened?

Two tasks that wait on each other ([`allSettled`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L2089-L2094)) both stay [`waiting`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L1585-L1591) forever, even after reopen. Nothing reports it: the tasks look like ordinary waiting work, the conversation never goes idle, and only [`abortTask`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L868-L877) frees them. As tasks wait on subagents and other tasks, a cycle like this can come from ordinary task code.

[`#validateWait`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/src/harness/scheduler.ts#L1003-L1025) already rejects a wait on the task itself or its owner chain, because those "could never finish first" ([§5.1](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L1735-L1737)). The same reason covers any cycle in the wait-for graph, where an edge T → U means "T can't finish before U". There are two kinds, and a cycle can mix them:

- T is `waiting` with U in its `on`
- U is live ordinary owned work of T, so T holds as [`completing`](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L1592-L1593) until U is done ([§5.5](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/4c6fb7cfe8c538a668726f6f8b3554098c39faee/packages/durable/docs/spec.md?plain=1#L2054-L2061), the nested-transaction rule)

But currently only the two shortest cycles are checked:

| cycle | today |
|---|---|
| a waits on a | rejected |
| a child waits on its owner | rejected |
| a waits on b, b waits on a | hangs |
| a cycle through a held owner (below) | hangs |

### Steps to reproduce

```
               owns (holds)
  orchestrator ────────────▶ worker
       ▲                       │ waits
       │ waits                 ▼
       └──────────────── reviewer
```

The less obvious case mixes the two, and each step is reasonable on its own:

1. An orchestrator spawns a worker task, then returns its result. The worker is still running, so the orchestrator holds as `completing` until it finishes.
2. A reviewer waits for the orchestrator's final result.
3. The worker waits for the reviewer's feedback before finishing.

None of the three can finish first, and all three hang. Longer plain cycles (x→y→z→x) hang too.

<details><summary>Tests (append to <code>test/harness-structured.test.ts</code>; both time out)</summary>

```ts
it("rejects a wait cycle", async () => {
	const { harness, root } = await openNodes();
	let a: TaskId | undefined;
	let b: TaskId | undefined;
	script("a", { run: (runtime, ctx) => runtime.commit(() => waitOn([b!], "allSettled"), ctx) });
	script("b", { run: (runtime, ctx) => runtime.commit(() => waitOn([a!], "allSettled"), ctx) });
	await root.commit(async (tx) => {
		a = await tx.createTask(Node, { name: "a" }, OWN_CONVERSATION);
		b = await tx.createTask(Node, { name: "b" }, OWN_CONVERSATION);
	}, context);
	const outcomes = await Promise.all([outcomeOf(harness, a!), outcomeOf(harness, b!)]);
	expect(outcomes.sort()).toEqual(["completed", "faulted"]);
	await harness.close(context);
});

it("rejects a wait cycle through a completing hold", async () => {
	const { harness, root } = await openNodes();
	const ids: Record<string, TaskId> = {};
	const reviewerWaits = deferred();

	// 1. The orchestrator spawns a worker, then returns its result in its next phase (the default `resume`). The worker
	//    is still running, so the orchestrator holds as `completing`: orchestrator → worker.
	script("orchestrator", {
		run: (runtime, ctx) =>
			runtime.commit(async (tx) => {
				ids.worker = await spawn(tx, runtime.taskId, "worker");
				return { status: "running", checkpoint: { phase: "resume", round: 1 } };
			}, ctx),
	});
	// 2. The reviewer waits for the orchestrator's final result: reviewer → orchestrator.
	script("reviewer", {
		run: async (runtime, ctx) => {
			await runtime.commit(() => waitOn([ids.orchestrator!], "allSettled"), ctx);
			reviewerWaits.resolve();
		},
	});
	// 3. The worker waits for the reviewer's feedback before finishing: worker → reviewer, closing the cycle.
	script("worker", {
		run: async (runtime, ctx) => {
			await reviewerWaits.promise;
			await runtime.commit(() => waitOn([ids.reviewer!], "allSettled"), ctx);
		},
	});

	ids.orchestrator = await start(root, "orchestrator");
	await until(async () => (await state(harness, ids.orchestrator!)).status === "completing");
	ids.reviewer = await start(root, "reviewer");

	// Today all three hang. Expected: the worker's wait is rejected, faulting it; then the orchestrator and reviewer finish.
	const outcomes = await Promise.all(
		["orchestrator", "worker", "reviewer"].map((name) => outcomeOf(harness, ids[name]!)),
	);
	expect(outcomes).toEqual(["completed", "faulted", "completed"]);
	await harness.close(context);
});
```
</details>

### Expected behavior

The wait that closes a cycle faults, like a wait on the owner already does. A check has to follow both kinds of edge: `on` edges alone miss the cycle above. Happy to send a fix.

Refs: Holt, ["Some Deadlock Properties of Computer Systems"](https://doi.org/10.1145/356603.356607), 1972 (wait-for graphs); Moss, [*Nested Transactions*](https://ftp.mirrorservice.org/sites/www.bitsavers.org/pdf/mit/lcs/tr/MIT-LCS-TR-0260.pdf), 1981.

